# nexus-update sudoers entry — installed to /etc/sudoers.d/nexus-update
#
# Phase 9 (M_OTA) + Phase 10 (runtime-dep auto-install). Grants the
# unprivileged `nexus` service user exactly ONE privileged command: the pinned,
# root-owned OTA applier at /usr/local/sbin/nexus-apply-release.
#
# WHY A SINGLE STABLE GRANT (and not per-command argv rules).
# ----------------------------------------------------------
# This file used to whitelist the *exact argv* of four separate commands
# (`tar`, `ln`, `systemctl --no-block restart`, `nexus-apply-deps`). That
# coupled these bytes to the engine's `std::process::Command` calls: any drift
# — a new flag, a reordered arg, or simply an OLD copy of this file left on a
# box — made sudo refuse the command and BRICKED the OTA (an OTA cannot rewrite
# its own sudoers, which lives outside the nexus-writable tree). A real
# postmortem: an old sudoers line missing `--no-block` rejected the restart, so
# BOTH the apply and the rollback failed → `failed:rollback_also_failed`, and
# the box silently kept running the deleted old binary.
#
# The grant below is now argv-independent and FROZEN. All privileged OTA
# behaviour lives in the fixed, audited root-owned wrapper
# /usr/local/sbin/nexus-apply-release (extract → deps/journald → symlink flip →
# restart, plus `reflip` for rollback and post-health `prune`). The engine's
# privileged behaviour can evolve freely without ever touching this file again;
# the signed release supplies only DATA, never new argv. Changing the privileged
# surface is a deliberate edit to the wrapper, adopted via one install.sh run.
#
# Security scope:
#   - Pinned to a SINGLE absolute path outside the nexus-writable /opt/nexus
#     tree, so a compromised engine cannot swap the wrapper.
#   - The trailing `*` matches the subcommand + its argument
#     (`apply <version>`, `reflip <version>`, `prune <version>`, and the
#     argument-less remote-shell modes `ssh-ca-install`,
#     `ssh-principals-sync`, `sshd-restart`). sudo's `*` does NOT cross `/`,
#     so no path argument can be smuggled through — which is exactly why the
#     ssh modes read their input from fixed engine-writable staging paths
#     under /var/lib/nexus/ssh/ rather than taking a filename. The wrapper
#     additionally validates the version charset, rejects path traversal, and
#     charset-validates every staged principal before doing anything.
#   - Adding these modes required NO edit to the grant below. That is the
#     whole point of the argv-independent wildcard: privileged behaviour
#     evolves in the audited root-owned wrapper, never in sudoers.
#   - The wrapper enforces its own apt allowlist and `rm -rf` keep-set, so this
#     rule grants NO general `apt`, `systemctl`, `tar`, or `rm` power.
#
# The bytes of the artifact never transit this boundary — the engine downloads
# + verifies (Ed25519 signature + sha256) them as the `nexus` user and stages
# the tarball; the wrapper only touches already-staged, already-verified files.
#
# Install:
#   sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g root deploy/sudoers.d/nexus-update \
#       /etc/sudoers.d/nexus-update
#   sudo visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/nexus-update   # validate
nexus ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/nexus-apply-release *
